# ══════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Dependencies
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════
node_modules/

# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored (Sprint 7, 2026-08-04).
#
# It was, and that had two costs. CI cannot run `npm ci` without it, so there is
# no way to check a pull request against the dependency tree it actually ships
# with. And every install re-resolves from scratch, so nobody's node_modules is
# the same as anybody else's — which is exactly how Sprint 4 ended up with npm
# quietly deduping `source-map` to a hoisted 0.5.7 with a different, synchronous
# API than the 0.7.4 the code was written against.
#
# Ignoring a lockfile is a reasonable choice for a published library, whose
# consumers resolve their own tree. This is an application. It should install the
# same way twice.

